UPDATE lessons SET title='Year 7, Lesson 6: The design process', data=json_patch(data,'{"child_mission":"Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept. A trade-off is a benefit you give up so a more important criterion is met. Jordan’s checks are still: holds 8 pencils, fits a 15 cm by 15 cm corner, and has no sharp edges. The flat tray and the 18 cm sleeve struggle with the corner because the pencil is longer than 15 cm. The standing roll can meet the count and the corner, but a narrow roll tips. That loss of stability is the trade-off, not a reason to hide the weakness. “I like it” does not explain a choice.","coming_soon":null,"duration_minutes":55,"evidence_instructions":"Save the independent response, specific revisions and both short-answer explanations; record oral evidence for speaking tasks.","explicit_teaching":null,"extension":"Apply the same technology/design idea to a new object or material, then compare two pieces of evidence for strength.","follow_up_challenges":null,"guided_practice":"PRACTISE BEFORE READING THE FEEDBACK\nChoose one of the tray, the sleeve or the standing roll for Jordan. Say which of the three criteria it can meet, and name one trade-off you accept. The pencil measure in the model is 18 cm and the corner is 15 cm by 15 cm.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChoose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth. The tray and the sleeve are about as long as the 18 cm pencil, so they miss the corner. The trade-off is stability: a narrow roll tips more easily than a flat tray. Saying the roll “looks cool” does not show the criteria or the weakness you accept.\n\nWATCH FOR\nPicking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all. Every idea gives something up. Hiding the weakness makes the next test meaningless. Name the criteria the idea can meet and state the trade-off in one clear sentence.","hoard_words":null,"independent_task":"INDEPENDENT EVIDENCE\nChoose between a flat pocket and a small box for a bus pass and a library card kept together. Criteria: fits a pocket, keeps both cards dry, and has no sharp edge. Explain one trade-off. Do not choose the pencil roll or reuse the 15 cm desk corner.\n\nWrite a short reflection naming one observation, criterion or step you corrected and why.","interactive_activities":null,"learning_area":"Design and Technology","learning_intention":"Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept. A trade-off is a benefit you give up so a more important criterion is met. Jordan’s checks are still: holds 8 pencils, fits a 15 cm by 15 cm corner, and has no sharp edges. The flat tray and the 18 cm sleeve struggle with the corner because the pencil is longer than 15 cm. The standing roll can meet the count and the corner, but a narrow roll tips. That loss of stability is the trade-off, not a reason to hide the weakness. “I like it” does not explain a choice.","lesson_number":6,"lesson_role":"Apply","materials":["Paper or typed answer space","Pencil","Optional: safe classroom tools/materials under teacher supervision","Device only when the lesson requires digital work"],"offline_alternative":"Use paper and pencil to complete the original task, then retain a photograph or short parent observation as evidence.","outcome_codes":["DIT5-EVL-01"],"parent_notes":"Parent: Marking guidance for the independent task: Choose between a flat pocket and a small box for a bus pass and a library card kept together. Criteria: fits a pocket, keeps both cards dry, and has no sharp edge. Explain one trade-off. Do not choose the pencil roll or  Accept equivalent original responses that show the same skill.\n\nParent: Written model 1: Sample: original example using trade-off on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.\n\nParent: Written model 2: Sample error: Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all.. Correction: Choose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth.. Why it helps the audience is stated.\n\nParent: Likely misconception: Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all. Every idea gives something up. Hiding the weakness makes the next test meaningless. Name the criteria the idea can meet and state the trade-off in one clear sentence. Keep sample answers out of the child role.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 1: Produce a fresh example demonstrating trade-off on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.\n\nParent: Short-answer focus 2: Name one likely error with trade-off, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.\n\nParent: Marking notes: Accept original work about trade-off with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material. / Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for trade-off. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea..","pass":1,"pass_mark":0.9,"planner_fields":null,"planner_intro":null,"planner_title":null,"printable_resource":null,"quiz":[{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"This matches the taught method for trade-off. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all","Ignore the observation or criteria and invent a more exciting result","Copy the model objects and call that independent work","Choose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth"],"question":"Choose one of the tray, the sleeve or the standing roll for Jordan. Say which of the three criteria it can meet, and name one trade-off you accept. The pencil measure in the model is 18 cm and the corner is 15 cm by 15 cm. Which response is most accurate?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"This is the misconception to avoid for trade-off; the other choices align with the lesson method. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Careful use of choice with clear evidence","Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all","Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept","Choose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth"],"question":"Which statement is a common mistake when working with trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Understanding is shown by new evidence and a named choice, not by copying the model. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all","Name choice only, without linking it to trade-off","Apply trade-off to new material and name the choice used","Repeat the worked example wording with no new evidence"],"question":"Which response best applies trade-off with new evidence?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"The stronger choice uses design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence appropriately for this lesson on trade-off. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all","Choose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which response best evaluates design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence when analysing trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Strong answers name specific evidence and what it shows. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name a criterion, safety step or test result and say how it improves trade-off","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing","Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all"],"question":"Which evidence move best supports a claim about trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"The stronger choice matches the taught idea (Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the) with concrete detail. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["The standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner","Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all","Start making with no criteria or safety check","Call the first attempt finished without testing"],"question":"Which choice best applies this lesson idea about trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":0,"explanation":"Accuracy means checking evidence and honouring limits or protocols. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Check criteria and safety, record a test result, and note one honest improvement","Invent missing details to make the answer sound complete","Ignore safety or cultural protocols to finish faster","Treat every claim as equally reliable without checking a source or cue"],"question":"Which action keeps design criteria, safe making steps or test evidence accurate for trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Independence requires a new example that still uses the taught skill. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["Name choice only, with no linked example","Choose between a flat pocket and a small box for a bus pass and a library card kept together","Retype the worked example with no new example","Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all"],"question":"Which independent response best applies trade-off to new material?","type":"multiple_choice"},{"explanation":"Independent evidence on new material demonstrates the skill; copying the model does not.","marking_guide":"Accept original work about trade-off with a specific supporting detail. Reject copied model wording with no new material.","question":"Produce a fresh example demonstrating trade-off on new material (not the worked model). Explain the particular words, sources or choices that make it work.","sample_answer":"Sample: original example using trade-off on new material; names a specific word, source or choice and explains its effect.","type":"short_answer"},{"explanation":"A useful correction changes the inaccurate idea or method and explains why.","marking_guide":"Accept a misconception, a correction, and a purpose-linked reason for trade-off. Reject corrections that do not change the inaccurate idea.","question":"Name one likely error with trade-off, correct it, and explain why the correction helps a reader or audience.","sample_answer":"Sample error: Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all.. Correction: Choose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth.. Why it helps the audience is stated.","type":"short_answer"}],"resources":null,"response_prompt":"Include your independent evidence and reflection, with reasons where requested.","review_status":"unreviewed","seed_key":"s5-design-technology-y9-w03-l2","sort_activity":null,"source_note":"Catalogue subject is Design and Technology. Proposed Year 7 alignment uses Design Innovation Technology 7–10 (2025) outcome DIT5-EVL-01, which the syllabus says may be adjusted for Stage 4 (DIT4-ADJ-01). The 2019 Design and Technology syllabus is in teach-out until 2029. Content-point mapping pending. Not NESA verified. Practical work is paper or cardboard only.","spelling":null,"spelling_focus":null,"stage":"S4","stage_hints":{"accept":["Today you will practise trade-off.","Read the mission before you start observing, designing or testing.","Keep the property, criterion or variable you are studying in view."],"guided":["Attempt the guided prompt without looking ahead.","Afterwards, compare your evidence with the feedback.","Adjust anything that does not match the method."],"learn":["Study how the first demonstration is built.","Point to the observation, criterion or step that carries the idea.","Then write the reason without copying every sentence."],"quiz":["Read every option before you choose.","Eliminate answers that ignore observations, criteria or fair tests.","Prefer accurate evidence over a guess that merely looks neat."],"submit":["Confirm that your evidence and reason are both present.","Proofread labels, measurements and sequence of steps.","Make sure the final evidence matches the lesson purpose."],"teach0":["Read the stage title and find the worked model.","Name one exact observation, criterion or test step before you decide.","Try the small prompt before opening the comparison."],"teach1":["Return to the model and list the key observations or criteria.","Say what each observation or criterion shows about the idea.","A final label alone is weaker than recorded evidence."],"teach2":["Compare a weak claim with stronger evidence.","Change the claim or method, not only how neat the drawing looks.","Name the mistake you are repairing."],"teach3":["Choose a fresh object or context that still uses the same skill.","Write one reason in your own words.","Do not retype the worked model as your answer."],"write":["Follow every part of the independent instructions.","Include original evidence and a short reason.","Save work that shows the skill on new objects or data."]},"subject":"Design and Technology","success_criteria":["I can use trade-off with a specific example from this lesson.","I can identify choice and explain why it matters.","I can practise stability independently on a new prompt.","I can notice compromise needs and correct a likely mistake."],"suspense_builder":null,"teach_steps":[{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner; stability is the trade-off. Tray: folded smooth edges and a low shape, but an 18 cm pencil lying flat sticks out of the 15 cm corner, so corner fit fails. Sleeve: holds eight pencils in a row and edges can be folded, but it is about 18 cm long, so it also misses the corner. Standing roll: pencils stand in a tube with a base under 15 cm, and edges can be folded smooth, so count and corner can pass. It tips if the base is narrow. The accepted trade-off is less stability, to be improved later with a wider base, not ignored.\n\nTRY FIRST\nChoose one of the tray, the sleeve or the standing roll for Jordan. Say which of the three criteria it can meet, and name one trade-off you accept. The pencil measure in the model is 18 cm and the corner is 15 cm by 15 cm.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChoose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth. The tray and the sleeve are about as long as the 18 cm pencil, so they miss the corner. The trade-off is stability: a narrow roll tips more easily than a flat tray. Saying the roll “looks cool” does not show the criteria or the weakness you accept.","explain":"Choosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept. A trade-off is a benefit you give up so a more important criterion is met. Jordan’s checks are still: holds 8 pencils, fits a 15 cm by 15 cm corner, and has no sharp edges. The flat tray and the 18 cm sleeve struggle with the corner because the pencil is longer than 15 cm. The standing roll can meet the count and the corner, but a narrow roll tips. That loss of stability is the trade-off, not a reason to hide the weakness. “I like it” does not explain a choice.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all. Every idea gives something up. Hiding the weakness makes the next test meaningless. Name the criteria the idea can meet and state the trade-off in one clear sentence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Understand and model","visual":[{"alt":"An 18 cm flat pencil missing a 15 cm corner beside a standing roll that fits.","caption":"The corner criterion eliminates the long flat ideas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Corner miss</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">18 cm pencil; 15 cm space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Roll fits</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Stands inside the corner</text></g></svg>"}],"visual_before":[{"episode":{"scenes":[{"bg":"room","lines":[{"text":"I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.","who":"pip"},{"text":"Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.","who":"juno"},{"text":"I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.","who":"pip"},{"pause_after":true,"text":"Pause here. Use this model: The standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner; stability is the trade-off. Tray: folde Then write your own evidence.","who":"juno"}]}],"title":"Talk through the first model","transcript":"Pip: I can see the example, but I am not sure which observation or criterion matters most.\nJuno: Name the observations, criteria or test steps that carry the idea before you decide.\nPip: I will try the prompt myself, then compare with the feedback.\nJuno: Pause here. Use this model: The standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner; stability is the trade-off. Tray: folde Then write your own evidence."}}]},{"check":{"correct_index":2,"explanation":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nThe standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner; stability is the trade-off. Tray: folded smooth edges and a low shape, but an 18 cm pencil lying flat sticks out of the 15 cm corner, so corner fit fails. Sleeve: holds eight pencils in a row and edges can be folded, but it is about 18 cm long, so it also misses the corner. Standing roll: pencils stand in a tube with a base under 15 cm, and edges can be folded smooth, so count and corner can pass. It tips if the base is narrow. The accepted trade-off is less stability, to be improved later with a wider base, not ignored. Look at each observation, source detail or example that carries the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nList the key observations, sources or steps from the model and say what each one shows.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe most useful record names the evidence and the reason it supports the claim, not only a label.","explain":"Make the technology idea visible: name the observation, source or example you used and say why it supports the claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Writing only a final label without evidence does not show technology thinking. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Show the evidence","visual":[{"alt":"The chosen standing roll beside the stability trade-off of a narrow base.","caption":"A choice names both the criteria met and the weakness accepted.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Chosen idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Standing roll holds eight</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Trade-off</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Narrow roll can tip</text></g></svg>"}]},{"check":{"correct_index":1,"explanation":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A guess with no evidence","Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison","A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nPicking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all. Every idea gives something up. Hiding the weakness makes the next test meaningless. Name the criteria the idea can meet and state the trade-off in one clear sentence. Here is a more productive model: The standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner; stability is the trade-off. Tray: folded smooth edges and a low shape, but an 18 cm pencil lying flat sticks out of the 15 cm corner, so corner fit fails. Sleeve: holds eight pencils in a row and edges can be folded, but it is about 18 cm long, so it also misses the corner. Standing roll: pencils stand in a tube with a base under 15 cm, and edges can be folded smooth, so count and corner can pass. It tips if the base is narrow. The accepted trade-off is less stability, to be improved later with a wider base, not ignored.\n\nTRY FIRST\nDescribe why the warned-against claim fails, then write a corrected claim with evidence.\n\nFEEDBACK\nChoose the standing roll: it can hold eight upright pencils in the 15 cm corner, and edges can be folded smooth. The tray and the sleeve are about as long as the 18 cm pencil, so they miss the corner. The trade-off is stability: a narrow roll tips more easily than a flat tray. Saying the roll “looks cool” does not show the criteria or the weakness you accept.","explain":"Use an intentionally incorrect claim as a comparison. Name its specific technology error, then show the corrected evidence-based claim.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Changing the wording without repairing the evidence does not fix the error. Picking the prettiest idea and pretending the chosen design has no disadvantage at all. Every idea gives something up. Hiding the weakness makes the next test meaningless. Name the criteria the idea can meet and state the trade-off in one clear sentence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Test a misconception"},{"check":{"correct_index":3,"explanation":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence. Supported records follow the actual observations, criteria and purpose of the task. The other choices miss a key step or use a common mistake.","options":["A claim unrelated to the observation, test or criteria","An answer copied without understanding","A guess with no evidence","Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example"],"question":"Which choice best shows accurate use of this idea about trade-off?","type":"multiple_choice"},"example":"WORKED MODEL\nChoosing an idea means matching it to the criteria and naming the trade-off you accept. A trade-off is a benefit you give up so a more important criterion is met. Jordan’s checks are still: holds 8 pencils, fits a 15 cm by 15 cm corner, and has no sharp edges. The flat tray and the 18 cm sleeve struggle with the corner because the pencil is longer than 15 cm. The standing roll can meet the count and the corner, but a narrow roll tips. That loss of stability is the trade-off, not a reason to hide the weakness. “I like it” does not explain a choice. Now choose a different example to show you can transfer the idea.\n\nTRY FIRST\nInquire into or describe one fresh example that uses the same skill and write a short evidence-based reason.\n\nFEEDBACK\nThe new example should use the same technology idea as the model, but contain different evidence.","explain":"Use the same technology idea on a new place, person, source or context, not a copy of the worked example. Review accuracy of evidence.\n\nWHY IT MATTERS\nThis helps the learner base claims on observation, fair tests and design evidence, rather than guessing from a picture alone. Keep every claim linked to what was seen, measured or tested against criteria.\n\nHOW TO WORK\nRead the worked example and name the observations, properties, criteria or test steps that carry the idea. Before looking at the correction, make your own attempt using the exact question provided. Compare your record with the feedback: did you use the same skill and show your evidence? If a result is uncertain, identify what would have to be observed or tested again. One deliberate, evidence-based revision is more useful than writing a longer answer. For practical technology work, follow teacher safety rules for tools, food and devices.","icon":"📖","notice":"Repeating the worked example cannot show transfer to new evidence. Correct the claim, criteria use or method, not just the neatness of the diagram.","title":"Transfer to a new investigation"}],"title":"Year 7, Lesson 6: The design process","unit_number":1,"unit_title":"The design process","week":5,"week_in_unit":3,"word_challenges":null,"worked_example":"WORK THROUGH THIS\nThe standing roll is chosen because it can hold eight pencils inside the corner; stability is the trade-off. Tray: folded smooth edges and a low shape, but an 18 cm pencil lying flat sticks out of the 15 cm corner, so corner fit fails. Sleeve: holds eight pencils in a row and edges can be folded, but it is about 18 cm long, so it also misses the corner. Standing roll: pencils stand in a tube with a base under 15 cm, and edges can be folded smooth, so count and corner can pass. It tips if the base is narrow. The accepted trade-off is less stability, to be improved later with a wider base, not ignored.\n\nWrite what makes this example accurate, naming the observations, criteria, properties or test steps used.","worked_visuals":[{"alt":"An 18 cm flat pencil missing a 15 cm corner beside a standing roll that fits.","caption":"The corner criterion eliminates the long flat ideas.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Corner miss</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">18 cm pencil; 15 cm space</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Roll fits</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Stands inside the corner</text></g></svg>"},{"alt":"The chosen standing roll beside the stability trade-off of a narrow base.","caption":"A choice names both the criteria met and the weakness accepted.","svg":"<svg xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/2000/svg\" viewBox=\"0 0 660 150\"><rect x=\"10\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#fff4ea\" stroke=\"#b87445\"/><rect x=\"340\" y=\"12\" width=\"310\" height=\"126\" rx=\"14\" fill=\"#e8f5ed\" stroke=\"#297a61\"/><g font-size=\"16\" fill=\"#263948\"><text x=\"28\" y=\"42\">Chosen idea</text><text x=\"28\" y=\"78\">Standing roll holds eight</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"42\">Trade-off</text><text x=\"358\" y=\"78\">Narrow roll can tip</text></g></svg>"}],"year_level":"Year 7"}') WHERE seed_key='s5-design-technology-y9-w03-l2' AND family_id IS NULL;
